## Solution

For this challenge, I followed the bit-packing
approach from the uploaded suggested solution.

The main idea is to combine the three fields
of the Obj structure into a single 64-bit integer
rather than storing each field separately.

This could reduce memory usage for each
heap-allocated object.

However, it also introduces additional work
when reading or updating the individual fields.

## 1. Understanding the Original Obj Structure

In Chapter 26, the Obj structure is defined
in object.h as:

```c
struct sObj {
  ObjType type;
  bool isMarked;
  struct Obj* next;
};
```

Each field serves an important purpose.

The type field identifies what kind of object
is being stored, such as a string, function,
closure, or upvalue.

The isMarked field is used during garbage
collection to record whether an object
is reachable.

The next pointer connects objects in the
VM's linked list of heap-allocated objects.

Together, these fields form the header
that every clox object contains.

## 2. How Much Memory Does the Header Use?

According to the uploaded suggested solution,
the original Obj structure occupies 16 bytes
on the author's 64-bit Mac.

Although the fields do not necessarily use
16 bytes of actual data, the C compiler may
insert padding to satisfy memory alignment
requirements.

For example, on a typical system with
a four-byte enum and an eight-byte pointer:

```text
ObjType type       4 bytes
bool isMarked      1 byte
Padding            3 bytes
Obj* next          8 bytes
--------------------------
Total             16 bytes
```

This is a typical memory layout, not a
measurement from my own computer.

The actual layout depends on the compiler,
target architecture, and alignment rules.

## 3. Measuring the Original Header Size

To determine the actual size on a specific
machine, I would compile and run the following
small C program.

The example assumes access to the clox
object.h header.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#include "object.h"

int main(void) {
  printf("Size of Obj: %zu bytes\n",
         sizeof(Obj));

  printf("Offset of type: %zu\n",
         offsetof(Obj, type));

  printf("Offset of isMarked: %zu\n",
         offsetof(Obj, isMarked));

  printf("Offset of next: %zu\n",
